     "content": "### 1. Financial Evaluation & Reality Check\n\n#### Offer vs. Current Compensation (Annualized)\n* **Current Meta (IC6):** $240k base + $36k bonus + $250k equity = **~$526k/yr**\n* **Google Offer (L6):** $235k base + $47k bonus (20%) + $270k equity ($1.08M / 4) = **~$552k/yr**\n\nOn paper, this is only a **~$26k (5%) increase in annualized target compensation**. \n\n#### Vesting Schedule Mechanics\nGoogle typically vests new-hire equity on a **front-loaded schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**:\n* **Year 1:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity (33%) = **~$638k**\n* **Year 2:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k equity + Y1 Refresh ($62.5k) = **~$701k**\n* **Year 3:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $237.6k equity (22%) + Refreshes ($125k) = **~$645k**\n* **Year 4:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $129.6k equity (12%) + Refreshes ($187.5k) = **~$599k**\n\nWhile the front-loading makes Year 1 and Year 2 lucrative, your base salary would actually be taking a small drop ($240k down to $235k), there is **zero sign-on bonus**, and you are walking away from $200k in near-term vesting Meta RSUs with no risk premium for switching companies.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is This a Good Offer?\n**It is a standard, middle-of-the-band initial offer for Google L6 PM in the Bay Area.** \n\nHowever, **it is not compelling enough to leave Meta as-is**, especially since:\n1. Google reached out to you (you were not looking).\n2. You are happy and performing well in your current role.\n3. A lateral move (Meta IC6 to Google L6) typically commands a 15\u201325% premium on annualized compensation plus a sign-on bonus to offset unvested equity and transition friction.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommendation: Negotiate\n\n**Do not accept as-is, but do not walk away yet.** \n\nGoogle almost always leaves room for negotiation on initial offers, particularly on equity and sign-on bonuses. Because you are content at Meta and hold high leverage, you are in a prime position to counter firmly.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For (Concrete Targets)\n\n| Component | Initial Offer | Counter Target | Justification / Band Reality |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000** | Band goes up to $271k. Your current Meta base is $240k; you should not take a base cut. |\n| **New-Hire RSUs (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr) | **$1,280,000** ($320k/yr) | Pushes equity toward the 75th percentile for L6 ($320k/yr). |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets the unvested equity you are walking away from at Meta and bridges the Year 1 transition. |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$638k (front-loaded) | **~$730k+** (including sign-on) | |\n\n---\n\n### 5. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone or email using the following talking points:\n\n1. **Clarify the Vesting Schedule:** First confirm if the grant follows Google\u2019s standard 33/33/22/12 schedule or equal quarterly vesting.\n2. **Anchor on Unvested Equity & Current Base:** State clearly that you are walking away from $200k+ in unvested Meta RSUs over the next 12 months and a higher base salary ($240k).\n3. **Provide Clear Closing Conditions:**\n\n> *\"I\u2019m really impressed with the team and the scope of the L6 role in SF. However, because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and am performing well at Meta, the current compensation isn't quite at the level that justifies the transition risk and leaving my unvested equity behind.*\n>\n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$250,000** to reflect my current baseline and level,*\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,280,000** over 4 years, and*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$90,000** to offset the stock I\u2019m forfeiting in my next 12 months at Meta.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign and accept the offer.\"*",
